{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Pg5vPDi9naPR2PUMnWu4Ns","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BXkqy8efe8DGFGbPHhFj42","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/worst/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12114,"cache_read_input_tokens":70426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12114,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"852f2cc5-5597-45a4-9c6c-5f99555de423"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BXkqy8efe8DGFGbPHhFj42","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{When Does Cross-Method Consensus Fail in Circuit Discovery?}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{\n16\t  Anonymous\n17\t}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tMechanistic interpretability relies on circuit discovery methods to identify computational subgraphs in neural networks, yet different methods often disagree on which edges constitute the ``true'' circuit. A natural remedy is cross-method consensus: retain only edges endorsed by multiple independent methods. We conduct a systematic empirical study of binary consensus scoring (CSS) across three algorithmic families---gradient attribution (EAP-IG), edge pruning (EP), activation patching (ACDC), and iterative feature removal (IFR)---on four tasks and four transformer models (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B). We find that CSS produces faithful, edge-efficient circuits \\emph{only} when constituent methods provide genuinely independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard similarity). In 5 of 6 task--model configurations, gradient-based methods exhibit systematic correlation (Jaccard $0.38$--$0.62$), causing CSS to degenerate. The sole positive result (IOI on GPT-2) is confounded by ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} binarization and zero-ablation evaluation bias. We further show that the correlation between method diversity and consensus utility, while directionally correct, is statistically non-significant ($\\rho = -0.54$, $p = 0.27$, $n=6$) and driven by a single influential data point. Our analysis identifies two concrete failure modes---method correlation collapse and binarization-induced confounds---and offers practical guidelines for when consensus-based circuit validation can and cannot be trusted.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\n30\tCircuit discovery in transformers aims to identify minimal subgraphs---sets of edges between attention heads and MLPs---that are sufficient to explain a model's behavior on a given task \\citep{conmy2023towards,wang2023interpretability}. Multiple methods have been proposed, including gradient-based attribution \\citep{syed2023attribution}, edge pruning \\citep{li2024edge}, and activation patching \\citep{conmy2023towards}. These methods often identify substantially different circuits for the same task, raising a fundamental question: which edges are ``real''?\n31\t\n32\tCross-method consensus offers an intuitive answer: edges identified by multiple independent methods are more likely to be causally important. We formalize this as \\textbf{Consensus Support Scoring} (CSS), where each edge receives a score $s \\in \\{0, 1/K, 2/K, \\ldots, 1\\}$ reflecting the fraction of $K$ methods that include it. The CSS$=1.0$ subset---edges endorsed by all methods---should, in principle, constitute a high-confidence core circuit.\n33\t\n34\tThis paper investigates when this intuition holds and when it breaks down. We conduct experiments across 6 task--model pairs using up to $K=3$ methods from different algorithmic families. Our central finding is negative: \\textbf{CSS fails in the majority of configurations because current circuit discovery methods are not sufficiently independent.} Gradient-based methods (EAP-IG, EP) share underlying gradient information, producing correlated edge rankings. Even IFR, nominally from a different algorithmic family, exhibits high correlation with gradient methods on transformer architectures.\n35\t\n36\tWe identify two specific confounds that inflate apparent consensus utility:\n37\t\\begin{enumerate}\n38\t    \\item \\textbf{ACDC binarization pathology}: ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} thresholding produces extremely sparse circuits (14--35 edges) that artificially lower pairwise Jaccard, making methods appear more independent than they are.\n39\t    \\item \\textbf{Zero-ablation bias}: Zero ablation disproportionately penalizes larger circuits, inflating the edge-efficiency advantage of small consensus subsets.\n40\t\\end{enumerate}\n41\t\n42\tThese findings have practical implications: researchers should verify method independence (via pairwise Jaccard) before trusting consensus-based circuit validation, and should use mean or resample ablation rather than zero ablation for faithfulness evaluation.\n43\t\n44\t\\section{Background}\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{Circuit Discovery Methods}\n47\t\n48\tWe study four circuit discovery methods spanning three algorithmic families:\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{EAP-IG (Edge Attribution Patching with Integrated Gradients).} A gradient-based method that scores each edge by the integrated gradient of the model's output with respect to that edge's activation \\citep{syed2023attribution}. Computationally efficient (single backward pass with integration)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"75a6fe66-241d-47db-b62d-4a631631d682","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:35:37.700Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"abcee50fcbc787b50","tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/worst/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":84802,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":63858},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"faf99bb4-a129-4d59-8b37-4a6cc2851d47","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KHiFJqPBrZyf8bC2M8vF48","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G6dEwuo2bHzJJtBHmLGHFr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/worst/paper.tex","offset":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1491,"cache_read_input_tokens":82540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1491,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"a4a805d9-4847-44a0-8401-15dee5ac98dc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G6dEwuo2bHzJJtBHmLGHFr","type":"tool_result","content":"50\t\\paragraph{EAP-IG (Edge Attribution Patching with Integrated Gradients).} A gradient-based method that scores each edge by the integrated gradient of the model's output with respect to that edge's activation \\citep{syed2023attribution}. Computationally efficient (single backward pass with integration).\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{EP (Edge Pruning).} An iterative pruning method that removes edges in order of a pruning criterion derived from gradient information \\citep{li2024edge}. While operationally different from EAP-IG, both methods rely on backward-pass gradient information.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{ACDC (Automatic Circuit DisCovery).} An activation patching method that iteratively tests each edge by replacing its activation with a corrupted counterfactual \\citep{conmy2023towards}. Produces binary circuits via thresholding (commonly \\texttt{isinf}).\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{IFR (Iterative Feature Removal).} A similarity-based method that evaluates edge importance by measuring how feature representations change when edges are removed. Nominally distinct from gradient methods.\n57\t\n58\t\\subsection{Consensus Support Scoring}\n59\t\n60\tGiven $K$ circuit discovery methods, each producing a binary circuit $C_k \\subseteq \\mathcal{E}$ over the edge set $\\mathcal{E}$, we define the consensus support score for edge $e$ as:\n61\t\\begin{equation}\n62\t    \\text{CSS}(e) = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\mathbf{1}[e \\in C_k]\n63\t\\end{equation}\n64\tThe CSS$=1.0$ circuit is $C_{\\text{cons}} = \\bigcap_{k=1}^{K} C_k$. We evaluate circuits via two faithfulness metrics under ablation: \\textbf{CPR} (Circuit Performance Recovery), measuring how much of the full model's performance the circuit recovers, and \\textbf{CMD} (Circuit Mean Deviation), measuring the average deviation from full model behavior.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Method Independence}\n67\t\n68\tWe measure pairwise method agreement via Jaccard similarity:\n69\t\\begin{equation}\n70\t    J(C_i, C_j) = \\frac{|C_i \\cap C_j|}{|C_i \\cup C_j|}\n71\t\\end{equation}\n72\tHigh Jaccard indicates method correlation; the effective number of independent methods $K_{\\text{eff}}$ is substantially less than $K$ when methods share algorithmic underpinnings.\n73\t\n74\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Tasks}\n77\t\n78\tWe evaluate on four tasks of varying circuit structure:\n79\t\\begin{itemize}\n80\t    \\item \\textbf{IOI} (Indirect Object Identification): A well-studied task with a known localized circuit in GPT-2 \\citep{wang2023interpretability}.\n81\t    \\item \\textbf{SVA} (Subject-Verb Agreement): Agreement resolution requiring syntactic processing.\n82\t    \\item \\textbf{Arithmetic} (Greater-than): Numerical comparison requiring distributed computation through MLP layers.\n83\t    \\item \\textbf{DocQA} (Factual Recall): Document-based question answering involving distributed information retrieval.\n84\t\\end{itemize}\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Models}\n87\t\n88\tWe test across four model scales: GPT-2 Small (117M, 12 layers, 12 heads), Qwen-2.5 0.5B (24 layers, 16 heads), Gemma-2 2B (26 layers, 16 heads), and Llama-3.1 8B (32 layers, 32 heads).\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}\n91\t\n92\tAll methods produce top-$k$ edge sets with $k = 100$ unless otherwise noted. ACDC additionally produces \\texttt{isinf}-thresholded circuits. We evaluate faithfulness under three ablation regimes: zero, mean, and resample ablation. We report CPR (higher is better) and CMD (lower is better).\n93\t\n94\t\\section{Results}\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{GPT-2 Small: CSS Fails on 3 of 4 Tasks}\n97\t\\label{sec:gpt2}\n98\t\n99\tWe first evaluate CSS with $K=3$ (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC) on GPT-2 Small across four tasks.\n100\t\n101\t\\paragraph{IOI: The sole positive result.} On IOI, ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} threshold produces a 14-edge circuit. The pairwise Jaccard between gradient methods and ACDC is low (EAP-IG$\\times$ACDC: 0.09, EP$\\times$ACDC: 0.06), while gradient methods themselves correlate moderately (EAP-IG$\\times$EP: 0.31). CSS$=1.0$ coincides with the 14-edge ACDC circuit and achieves CPR $= 0.109$ under zero ablation, exceeding all single methods (best: EAP-IG at $0.029$). This is the only configuration where CSS provides a clear advantage.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{SVA: Gradient correlation dominates.} Table~\\ref{tab:sva_jaccard} shows that EAP-IG and EP share 55\\% of their top-100 edges on SVA. ACDC \\texttt{isinf} retains only 19 edges, and CSS$=1.0$ reduces to this sparse set.\n104\t\n105\t\\begin{table}[h]\n106\t\\centering\n107\t\\caption{SVA pairwise Jaccard similarity (GPT-2 Small, $k=100$).}\n108\t\\label{tab:sva_jaccard}\n109\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n110\t\\toprule\n111\t & EAP-IG & EP & ACDC \\\\\n112\t\\midrule\n113\tEAP-IG & 1.00 & 0.55 & 0.14 \\\\\n114\tEP & 0.55 & 1.00 & 0.11 \\\\\n115\tACDC & 0.14 & 0.11 & 1.00 \\\\\n116\t\\bottomrule\n117\t\\end{tabular}\n118\t\\end{table}\n119\t\n120\t\\begin{table}[h]\n121\t\\centering\n122\t\\caption{SVA faithfulness comparison (mean ablation, $k=50$).}\n123\t\\label{tab:sva_faith}\n124\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n125\t\\toprule\n126\tMethod & Edges & CPR & CMD \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\tCSS$=1.0$ (\\texttt{isinf}) & 19 & 0.91 & 0.12 \\\\\n129\tCSS$=1.0$ ($k=50$) & 38 & 0.88 & 0.18 \\\\\n130\tEAP-IG ($k=50$) & 50 & \\textbf{0.95} & \\textbf{0.07} \\\\\n131\tEP ($k=50$) & 50 & 0.93 & 0.10 \\\\\n132\tACDC ($k=50$) & 50 & 0.87 & 0.19 \\\\\n133\t\\bottomrule\n134\t\\end{tabular}\n135\t\\end{table}\n136\t\n137\tCSS$=1.0$ at $k=50$ achieves CPR $= 0.88$, below EAP-IG's $0.95$ (Table~\\ref{tab:sva_faith}). The consensus discards 12 high-contribution edges unique to EAP-IG that EP misses due to differing sensitivity to indirect effects.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{DocQA: Distributed circuits amplify correlation.} On the factual recall task, EAP-IG$\\times$EP Jaccard rises to $0.62$---the highest observed---because distributed computation produces a flatter gradient landscape where all methods identify similar ``information highway'' pathways. CSS$=1.0$ achieves CPR $= 0.82$ versus EAP-IG's $0.91$ (Table~\\ref{tab:docqa}).\n140\t\n141\t\\begin{table}[h]\n142\t\\centering\n143\t\\caption{DocQA faithfulness comparison (mean ablation, $k=50$).}\n144\t\\label{tab:docqa}\n145\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n146\t\\toprule\n147\tMethod & Edges & CPR & CMD \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\tCSS$=1.0$ ($k=50$) & 42 & 0.82 & 0.25 \\\\\n150\tEAP-IG ($k=50$) & 50 & \\textbf{0.91} & \\textbf{0.13} \\\\\n151\tEP ($k=50$) & 50 & 0.88 & 0.17 \\\\\n152\tACDC ($k=50$) & 50 & 0.84 & 0.22 \\\\\n153\t\\bottomrule\n154\t\\end{tabular}\n155\t\\end{table}\n156\t\n157\t\\paragraph{Summary.} Across four GPT-2 tasks, CSS$=1.0$ outperforms single methods only on IOI. On SVA, Arithmetic, and DocQA, the best single method (EAP-IG) consistently surpasses the consensus circuit in absolute CPR.\n158\t\n159\t\\subsection{Scaling Beyond GPT-2: IFR Does Not Restore Independence}\n160\t\\label{sec:scaling}\n161\t\n162\tWe replace ACDC with IFR on three models (Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B) to test whether a non-gradient method restores consensus utility.\n163\t\n164\t\\paragraph{Qwen-2.5 0.5B.} On IOI, IFR correlates substantially with both gradient methods (EAP-IG$\\times$IFR: $0.52$, EP$\\times$IFR: $0.45$). CSS$=1.0$ (48 edges) achieves CPR $= 0.08$, slightly below EAP-IG's $0.10$ ($\\Delta = -0.02$). On Arithmetic, the pattern repeats: CSS$=1.0$ CPR $= 0.90$ versus EAP-IG's $0.97$ (Table~\\ref{tab:qwen}).\n165\t\n166\t\\begin{table}[h]\n167\t\\centering\n168\t\\caption{Qwen-2.5 0.5B IOI pairwise Jaccard ($k=100$).}\n169\t\\label{tab:qwen_jaccard}\n170\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n171\t\\toprule\n172\t & EAP-IG & EP & IFR \\\\\n173\t\\midrule\n174\tEAP-IG & 1.00 & 0.48 & 0.52 \\\\\n175\tEP & 0.48 & 1.00 & 0.45 \\\\\n176\tIFR & 0.52 & 0.45 & 1.00 \\\\\n177\t\\bottomrule\n178\t\\end{tabular}\n179\t\\end{table}\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{table}[h]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\caption{Qwen-2.5 0.5B faithfulness results (mean ablation).}\n184\t\\label{tab:qwen}\n185\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n186\t\\toprule\n187\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{IOI} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Arithmetic} \\\\\n188\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n189\tMethod & Edges & CPR & Edges & CPR \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\tCSS$=1.0$ & 48 & 0.08 & 52 & 0.90 \\\\\n192\tEAP-IG & 100 & \\textbf{0.10} & 100 & \\textbf{0.97} \\\\\n193\tEP & 100 & 0.06 & 100 & 0.94 \\\\\n194\tIFR & 100 & 0.07 & 100 & 0.92 \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\end{table}\n198\t\n199\t\\paragraph{Gemma-2 2B.} On IOI, pairwise Jaccard ranges from $0.40$ to $0.48$. CSS$=1.0$ (55 edges) achieves CPR $= 0.06$, compared to EAP-IG's $0.07$ ($\\Delta = -0.01$). The GPT-2 IOI exception does not transfer to larger models where the circuit is more distributed.\n200\t\n201\t\\paragraph{Llama-3.1 8B.} At this scale, $k=100$ covers only $\\sim$0.1\\% of the edge space. CSS$=1.0$ (60 edges) achieves CPR $= 0.04$ versus EAP-IG's $0.05$. All methods operate in an extremely undersampled regime, making consensus trivially similar to random intersection.\n202\t\n203\t\\begin{table}[h]\n204\t\\centering\n205\t\\caption{IOI faithfulness across model scales (mean ablation).}\n206\t\\label{tab:scale}\n207\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n208\t\\toprule\n209\tModel & CSS Edges & CSS CPR & Best Single CPR & $\\Delta$ & Max Jaccard \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\tGPT-2 Small & 14 & 0.109 & 0.029 & +0.080 & 0.31 \\\\\n212\tQwen-2.5 0.5B & 48 & 0.08 & 0.10 & $-$0.02 & 0.52 \\\\\n213\tGemma-2 2B & 55 & 0.06 & 0.07 & $-$0.01 & 0.48 \\\\\n214\tLlama-3.1 8B & 60 & 0.04 & 0.05 & $-$0.01 & 0.44 \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\end{table}\n218\t\n219\tTable~\\ref{tab:scale} summarizes the IOI scaling results. CSS advantage is positive only on GPT-2, where ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} binarization produces an anomalously sparse circuit. On all other models using IFR, the advantage is negative or negligible.\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{Confound Analysis: Why IOI on GPT-2 Is an Outlier}\n222\t\\label{sec:confound}\n223\t\n224\t\\paragraph{ACDC binarization drives Jaccard variation.} ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} threshold produces circuits of 14--35 edges depending on the task, far smaller than the $k=100$ used for gradient methods. This asymmetry artificially deflates pairwise Jaccard between ACDC and gradient methods. Table~\\ref{tab:binarization} shows that normalizing to top-$k$ increases Jaccard substantially.\n225\t\n226\t\\begin{table}[h]\n227\t\\centering\n228\t\\caption{Effect of ACDC binarization on Jaccard (GPT-2, EAP-IG$\\times$ACDC).}\n229\t\\label{tab:binarization}\n230\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n231\t\\toprule\n232\tTask & Jaccard (\\texttt{isinf}) & Jaccard (top-$k$) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n233\t\\midrule\n234\tIOI & 0.09 & 0.25 & +0.16 \\\\\n235\tArithmetic & 0.17 & 0.42 & +0.25 \\\\\n236\tSVA & 0.14 & 0.35 & +0.21 \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\t\\paragraph{Zero-ablation bias favors small circuits.} Table~\\ref{tab:ablation} reveals that IOI's CSS advantage of $+0.080$ under zero ablation shrinks to $+0.007$ under mean ablation and $+0.003$ under resample ablation. Zero ablation sets ablated edges to zero, disproportionately disrupting larger circuits (more pathways severed). This gives CSS$=1.0$'s 14-edge circuit an unfair efficiency advantage. On SVA and Arithmetic, CSS underperforms under all ablation methods.\n242\t\n243\t\\begin{table}[h]\n244\t\\centering\n245\t\\caption{CSS$=1.0$ advantage ($\\Delta$ CPR vs.\\ best single method) across ablation types.}\n246\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n247\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n248\t\\toprule\n249\tTask & Zero $\\Delta$ & Mean $\\Delta$ & Resample $\\Delta$ \\\\\n250\t\\midrule\n251\tIOI & +0.080 & +0.007 & +0.003 \\\\\n252\tSVA & $-$0.020 & $-$0.070 & $-$0.070 \\\\\n253\tArithmetic & $-$0.053 & $-$0.066 & $-$0.070 \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\t\\subsection{Binarization Sensitivity}\n259\t\\label{sec:binarization}\n260\t\n261\tWe sweep $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$ to test whether CSS faithfulness is sensitive to the binarization threshold. Table~\\ref{tab:ksweep} shows that CSS CPR is stable across $k$ values (std $< 0.02$ within each task). This rules out binarization choice as the cause of CSS failure---the failure is driven by method correlation, not threshold sensitivity.\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[h]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\caption{CSS$=1.0$ CPR stability across binarization $k$ (GPT-2, mean ablation).}\n266\t\\label{tab:ksweep}\n267\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n268\t\\toprule\n269\tTask & $k=50$ & $k=75$ & $k=100$ & $k=150$ & Stable? \\\\\n270\t\\midrule\n271\tIOI & 0.095 & 0.100 & 0.105 & 0.098 & Yes \\\\\n272\tSVA & 0.88 & 0.87 & 0.86 & 0.85 & Yes \\\\\n273\tArithmetic & 0.919 & 0.910 & 0.905 & 0.895 & Yes \\\\\n274\t\\bottomrule\n275\t\\end{tabular}\n276\t\\end{table}\n277\t\n278\t\\subsection{CIRCUS: Single-Method Consensus as Negative Control}\n279\t\\label{sec:circus}\n280\t\n281\tCIRCUS \\citep{parekh2026circus} constructs consensus within a single method by varying the binarization threshold, producing nested circuit sets ($k=50 \\subset k=100 \\subset k=150$) whose intersection is the most conservative cut. Table~\\ref{tab:circus} confirms this approach fails completely: CPR is negative on all three tasks (worse than a random circuit), because threshold variation within a single gradient landscape provides no independent information.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[h]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\caption{CIRCUS vs.\\ CSS faithfulness (GPT-2, zero ablation).}\n286\t\\label{tab:circus}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\tMethod & IOI CPR & SVA CPR & Arith CPR \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tCIRCUS & $-$0.108 & $-$0.150 & $-$0.120 \\\\\n292\tCSS$=1.0$ & +0.109 & $-$0.050 & $-$0.184 \\\\\n293\tBest single & +0.029 & $-$0.030 & $-$0.131 \\\\\n294\t\\bottomrule\n295\t\\end{tabular}\n296\t\\end{table}\n297\t\n298\t\\subsection{Jaccard--Utility Correlation}\n299\t\\label{sec:correlation}\n300\t\n301\tWe test whether method diversity (measured as max pairwise Jaccard) predicts CSS utility across all task--model pairs. Table~\\ref{tab:correlation_data} lists the six data points; Table~\\ref{tab:correlation_stats} reports correlation statistics.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[h]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Jaccard--CSS advantage across task--model pairs.}\n306\t\\label{tab:correlation_data}\n307\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n308\t\\toprule\n309\tTask $\\times$ Model & Max Jaccard & CSS $\\Delta$ & Confounds \\\\\n310\t\\midrule\n311\tIOI $\\times$ GPT-2 & 0.31 & +0.080 & ACDC \\texttt{isinf} + zero abl. \\\\\n312\tSVA $\\times$ GPT-2 & 0.55 & $-$0.070 & --- \\\\\n313\tArith $\\times$ GPT-2 & 0.695 & $-$0.066 & --- \\\\\n314\tIOI $\\times$ Qwen & 0.52 & $-$0.020 & IFR $\\approx$ gradient \\\\\n315\tArith $\\times$ Qwen & 0.58 & $-$0.070 & --- \\\\\n316\tIOI $\\times$ Gemma & 0.48 & $-$0.010 & --- \\\\\n317\t\\bottomrule\n318\t\\end{tabular}\n319\t\\end{table}\n320\t\n321\t\\begin{table}[h]\n322\t\\centering\n323\t\\caption{Correlation robustness analysis.}\n324\t\\label{tab:correlation_stats}\n325\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n326\t\\toprule\n327\tAnalysis & $\\rho$ & $p$-value & $n$ \\\\\n328\t\\midrule\n329\tAll points & $-$0.54 & 0.27 & 6 \\\\\n330\tRemove IOI $\\times$ GPT-2 & $-$0.30 & 0.62 & 5 \\\\\n331\tRemove ACDC tasks only & $-$0.10 & 0.87 & 3 \\\\\n332\t\\bottomrule\n333\t\\end{tabular}\n334\t\\end{table}\n335\t\n336\tThe Spearman correlation is $\\rho = -0.54$ ($p = 0.27$), statistically non-significant. Removing the sole positive data point (IOI $\\times$ GPT-2), which has Cook's $D > 1.0$, reduces the correlation to $\\rho = -0.30$ ($p = 0.62$). The Jaccard--utility relationship is a \\emph{motivated conjecture} rather than an empirical finding: max Jaccard $> 0.5$ appears to be a necessary condition for CSS failure, but $< 0.5$ is not sufficient for success (only one such point exists, and it is confounded).\n337\t\n338\t\\section{Discussion}\n339\t\n340\t\\subsection{Why Do Circuit Discovery Methods Correlate?}\n341\t\n342\tThe systematic correlation between EAP-IG and EP (Jaccard $0.38$--$0.62$ across settings) is unsurprising in retrospect: both rely on backward-pass gradient information. In GPT-2's 12-layer architecture, the gradient landscape for localized tasks has few dominant pathways, and both methods rank edges similarly by gradient magnitude. IFR's correlation with gradient methods (Jaccard $0.45$--$0.55$) is more concerning: it suggests that on current transformer architectures, feature similarity and gradient magnitude produce convergent edge importance rankings, consistent with the ``privileged basis'' phenomenon in residual streams.\n343\t\n344\t\\subsection{The IOI Exception}\n345\t\n346\tGPT-2's IOI circuit is exceptional in two ways: (1) ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} threshold produces an extremely sparse 14-edge circuit that happens to capture the core name-mover and induction heads, and (2) this sparsity creates artificial method independence (low Jaccard). The CSS$=1.0$ circuit inherits ACDC's output, and its apparent superiority is amplified by zero ablation's bias toward small circuits. Under mean ablation, the CSS advantage drops from $+0.080$ to $+0.007$---within noise.\n347\t\n348\t\\subsection{Fixed-$k$ Consensus Does Not Scale}\n349\t\n350\tAt 8B parameters, $k=100$ covers $\\sim$0.1\\% of the edge space. All methods are severely undersampled, and their top-100 sets become increasingly similar in terms of the \\emph{functional roles} they capture (induction heads, name movers) even when specific edge indices differ. Consensus at fixed $k$ degenerates into a trivial intersection artifact at scale.\n351\t\n352\t\\subsection{Practical Recommendations}\n353\t\n354\tBased on our findings, we offer the following guidelines:\n355\t\\begin{enumerate}\n356\t    \\item \\textbf{Check method independence first.} Compute pairwise Jaccard before trusting any consensus result. If max Jaccard $> 0.5$, consensus is unlikely to add value.\n357\t    \\item \\textbf{Use mean or resample ablation.} Zero ablation systematically favors smaller circuits, confounding faithfulness comparisons across circuit sizes.\n358\t    \\item \\textbf{Normalize ACDC output.} When including ACDC in a consensus, use top-$k$ normalization rather than \\texttt{isinf} thresholding to prevent binarization artifacts.\n359\t    \\item \\textbf{Report $K_{\\text{eff}}$.} The nominal number of methods $K$ overstates independence; report effective independence via average pairwise Jaccard.\n360\t\\end{enumerate}\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n363\t\n364\tOur study is limited to $n=6$ task--model pairs, which is underpowered for correlation analysis (Spearman requires $n \\approx 10$ for 80\\% power at $\\rho = 0.6$). We evaluate only edge-level circuits; node-level or subspace-level consensus may behave differently. The IFR implementation may not represent the full family of non-gradient methods; other approaches (e.g., causal scrubbing, probing-based methods) could provide greater independence.\n365\t\n366\t\\section{Related Work}\n367\t\n368\t\\citet{conmy2023towards} introduced ACDC for automated circuit discovery. \\citet{syed2023attribution} proposed EAP-IG as a scalable gradient-based alternative. \\citet{li2024edge} developed edge pruning with improved efficiency. Cross-method comparison has been explored by \\citet{mondorf2025cross}, who use score averaging within the gradient family; our work uses binary consensus across families and critically examines when this fails. CIRCUS \\citep{parekh2026circus} attempts single-method consensus via threshold diversity, which we show is ineffective. Concurrent work on ensemble circuit discovery \\citep{pie2026} uses iterative pruning within a single paradigm, subject to similar correlation concerns.\n369\t\n370\t\\section{Conclusion}\n371\t\n372\tCross-method consensus in circuit discovery is an appealing idea that fails in practice under current conditions. The core bottleneck is not the consensus algorithm itself---CSS is stable across binarization thresholds and robust to hyperparameters---but the lack of genuinely independent circuit discovery methods for transformers. Gradient-based methods dominate the field, and even nominally distinct approaches (IFR) converge to similar edge rankings on current architectures. Until the community develops methods with fundamentally different inductive biases, consensus-based validation should be applied with caution and accompanied by explicit independence verification via pairwise Jaccard analysis.\n373\t\n374\t\\bibliography{references}\n375\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n376\t\n377\t\\end{document}\n378\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"9348315a-67aa-4e9a-81ec-120212230651","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:35:40.972Z"}
