{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7dHsaV6qnbwBhhSjnprj8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Method paper 不可行。Gradient-based methods (EAP-IG, EP) 在 GPT-2\n2\t  上系统性高相关（Jaccard 0.50-0.70）， K=3 的有效独立方法数 K_eff≈1.5。IFR 未能打破这一退化——在 GPT-2 scale 上\n3\t  similarity-based attribution 收敛到 gradient proxy。唯一正面结果 IOI 是 outlier（14-edge ACDC\n4\t  强先验 + 极端 localized circuit）。 Jaccard-utility 关系仅有 3-4 个数据点且被 ACDC binarization confound\n5\t  驱动，去除 ACDC 后 K=2 consensus 无统计意义。重构为 analysis paper: ''When Does Cross-Method Consensus\n6\t  Fail in Circuit Discovery?'' 投 EMNLP Findings 或 workshop。\n7\t\n8\t  '\n9\timagined_experiments:\n10\t- name: GPT-2 SVA K=3 CSS\n11\t  plan_id: plan_004\n12\t  description: 'Subject-Verb Agreement on GPT-2 Small, K=3 (EAP-IG + EP + ACDC)。 验证\n13\t    CSS 在第三个任务上的表现。SVA 电路预期比 IOI 更 localized， 但 worst 档假设 gradient methods 在 GPT-2\n14\t    上的相关性是系统性的，不因任务而异。\n15\t\n16\t    '\n17\t  expected_results:\n18\t    pairwise_jaccard:\n19\t      eapig_x_ep: 0.55\n20\t      eapig_x_acdc: 0.14\n21\t      ep_x_acdc: 0.11\n22\t    max_pairwise_jaccard: 0.55\n23\t    three_way_ratio: 0.09\n24\t    circuit_sizes:\n25\t      eapig: 100\n26\t      ep: 100\n27\t      acdc: 19\n28\t    acdc_binarization: isinf (19 edges)\n29\t    css_distribution:\n30\t      1/3: 42"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019CcFb467rhLmpU9tcYdWVV","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"13f8dab3-baf9-4978-ba64-d4f439ec4906","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:35:46.940Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a7c0d32945c92e2a5","tool_use_id":"toolu_011DsfFpvo1Urbe6HV8guPNd","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":82920,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":129094},"uuid":"6677e0bd-414d-48ba-b8b3-bf260157d5e8","session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011DsfFpvo1Urbe6HV8guPNd","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information to write a thorough review. Let me now compose it based on the paper description provided in the prompt and all the project materials I've reviewed.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether binary Consensus Support Scoring (CSS) across multiple circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC, IFR) can identify more faithful circuits than any single method alone. The central finding is negative in the majority of configurations: CSS fails in 5 out of 6 task-model combinations because the underlying methods are not independent, as measured by pairwise Jaccard indices. Two specific confounds are identified: ACDC's `isinf` binarization producing degenerate edge sets, and zero-ablation bias inflating or deflating CPR measurements. A Jaccard-based meta-diagnostic is proposed but the supporting correlation (rho=-0.54, p=0.27, n=6) is not statistically significant.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Timely and important question.** The field has produced many circuit discovery methods but virtually no systematic study of when and why they agree or disagree. This paper fills a genuine gap.\n\n- **Honest negative result.** The paper does not oversell: CSS fails in 5/6 configurations, and this is stated upfront. Negative-result papers in interpretability are undervalued, and the community benefits from knowing that naive consensus does not work.\n\n- **Confound analysis is concrete and actionable.** The identification of ACDC `isinf` binarization as a degeneration mechanism (Table 7) is a genuinely useful methodological contribution. This will save other researchers from making the same mistake. The zero-ablation bias finding (Table 8) corroborates Miller et al. (COLM 2024) with new evidence.\n\n- **Binarization sensitivity analysis** (CSS stable across k) is well-executed and addresses an obvious concern.\n\n- **CIRCUS negative control** adds value by showing that within-method threshold diversity also fails, reinforcing that the problem is deeper than aggregation strategy.\n\n- **Clear experimental setup.** Four tasks spanning the localized-to-distributed spectrum, four models from 117M to 8B, three ablation regimes. The experimental matrix is well-designed for the question being asked.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **[MAJOR] The claimed Jaccard-utility relationship is not statistically significant.** The paper reports rho=-0.54, p=0.27 on n=6 data points. This is the would-be \"crown jewel\" of the paper (Claim 2), but it does not clear even a lenient significance threshold. Without this, Claim 2 reduces to a \"directional observation\" that cannot support the proposed Jaccard threshold of ~0.53 or the two-step practitioner workflow. The paper needs either (a) many more data points to establish statistical power, or (b) to honestly downgrade this to a hypothesis rather than a finding.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Novelty of the central negative finding is limited.** The claim \"consensus fails when methods are correlated\" is, at the level of principle, unsurprising and arguably tautological. Any ensemble/voting system degrades when voters are correlated -- this is well-established in statistics (Condorcet's jury theorem requires independence), machine learning (ensemble diversity theory: Krogh & Vedelsby 1994, Brown et al. 2005), and has been noted in the interpretability context by Meloux et al. (2025), who showed single-method Jaccard of only 0.56 under hyperparameter variation. The novelty must rest on the *specific empirical quantification* rather than the principle, but the quantification is hampered by the non-significant correlation (see above).\n\n- **[MAJOR] Related work section is severely incomplete.** All citations in Section 6 are placeholder `[?]` markers. Beyond this formatting issue, the substantive coverage is thin:\n  - **No discussion of ensemble diversity theory** from the broader ML literature. The Jaccard-as-independence-predictor idea maps directly to the diversity-accuracy decomposition in ensemble methods (Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003, \"Measures of Diversity in Classifier Ensembles and Their Relationship with the Ensemble Accuracy\"). This connection is absent.\n  - **Missing concurrent/recent circuit discovery benchmarks:** InterpBench (NeurIPS 2024 D&B), MIB (ICML 2025), Formal MI (Hadad et al., ICLR 2026) all provide relevant context on method evaluation and ground-truth circuits. InterpBench specifically found ACDC and EAP-IG are \"statistically indistinguishable\" on semi-synthetic models -- directly relevant to the method correlation finding.\n  - **Certified/robust circuit discovery** is unmentioned: Anani et al. (2026, \"Certified Circuits\") and Nikankin et al. (BlackboxNLP 2025) on bootstrap EAP-IG stability both address circuit robustness through different lenses.\n  - **Circuit non-uniqueness** (Huang et al., ICLR 2025, \"Everything Everywhere All at Once\"): if circuits are inherently non-unique, the theoretical basis for expecting cross-method consensus requires explicit discussion.\n  - **SAE-based and transcoder-based circuit discovery** (Marks et al., ICLR 2025 Oral; Dunefsky et al., NeurIPS 2024; Ameisen et al. 2025) represent an entirely different paradigm for circuit discovery at the feature level rather than edge level. These are not discussed, yet they change the landscape of what \"cross-method consensus\" could mean.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Framing tension: the paper describes itself as a paper proposing CSS, but the actual finding is that CSS mostly does not work.** The title references CSS as an analysis paper's finding, but the introduction still reads as a method contribution (contributions list CSS as the first item). This framing mismatch will confuse reviewers. If the paper is an analysis/negative-result paper, it should be framed as: \"We systematically study whether cross-method consensus helps in circuit discovery and find that it mostly does not, due to method correlation. We identify two confounds and propose diagnostic guidelines.\" If it is a method paper proposing CSS, it needs CSS to work more often than it fails.\n\n- **[MINOR] The paper's 4 practical recommendations need validation.** \"Check independence first\" and \"use mean/resample ablation\" and \"normalize ACDC\" and \"report K_eff\" are sensible suggestions, but they are presented as guidelines without demonstrating that following them actually improves downstream circuit analysis outcomes. At minimum, showing that the one successful case (IOI on GPT-2) would have been predicted by the guidelines, and the five failures would have been avoided, would strengthen this claim.\n\n- **[MINOR] IOI as the sole positive case is concerning.** IOI is the most studied circuit in the interpretability literature (Wang et al. 2023), and methods have been iteratively tuned on it. The one case where CSS works (IOI) may reflect this privileged status rather than a general property of \"localized circuits.\" SVA is mentioned in the paper structure but the statistical evidence is based on n=6 with IOI driving most of the positive signal.\n\n- **[MINOR] IFR's role is not well-justified.** IFR is introduced as a fourth algorithmic family to increase diversity, but its theoretical properties (scoring edges by cosine similarity changes upon feature removal) are not clearly in the same \"causal circuit\" framework as the other three methods. The paper should discuss whether adding a non-causal method to a causal consensus framework is principled or introduces apples-to-oranges comparisons.\n\n### Questions to Authors\n\n1. The reported correlation is rho=-0.54, p=0.27 on n=6. In the project's \"best-tier\" imagined experiments, the correlation is rho=0.94, p=0.005 on n=6. Which numbers reflect the actual experimental results? If the former, how do you justify Claim 2? If the latter, please clarify which experiments were actually run vs. simulated.\n\n2. Have you considered the connection to ensemble diversity theory (e.g., Kuncheva & Whitaker's diversity measures)? Your Jaccard-based meta-diagnostic is essentially a diversity measure for circuit discovery ensembles. Framing it in those terms could strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. The ACDC `isinf` binarization confound effectively means ACDC is using a fundamentally different selection criterion than the other methods. After normalizing to top-k, what is the \"true\" disagreement pattern? Does ACDC still contribute independent information, or does it become redundant with one of the gradient methods?\n\n4. Why not include CD-T (Hsu et al., ICLR 2025), which represents a decomposition-based family distinct from all four methods used? This could genuinely increase K_eff.\n\n5. For the CIRCUS negative control: you ran CIRCUS on EAP-IG only. CIRCUS was designed for cross-layer transcoder attribution graphs (Ameisen et al.), not for EAP-IG. Is the negative result a fair comparison, or does it reflect applying CIRCUS outside its intended domain?\n\n### Novelty Score: 3/6\n\n**Justification:** The core negative finding (consensus fails under method correlation) is important to document empirically but is not conceptually surprising. The confound analysis (ACDC `isinf`, zero-ablation bias) is the most novel component but could be a short paper. The Jaccard-utility relationship, which would be the main novelty differentiator, is not statistically significant with the current data. Mondorf et al. (2025) already explored cross-method aggregation (union/intersection/mean) on gradient-family methods. CIRCUS (Parekh 2026) already explored consensus within single methods. This paper fills a gap between them but the gap is narrower than presented.\n\n### Related Work Score: 2/6\n\n**Justification:** All citations in Section 6 are literal `[?]` placeholders, which is unacceptable even for a draft. Beyond formatting, the paper omits (a) the ensemble diversity literature that directly motivates the Jaccard diagnostic, (b) concurrent circuit discovery benchmarks (InterpBench, MIB) that contextualize method disagreement, (c) the circuit non-uniqueness literature that challenges the theoretical basis of consensus, (d) SAE/transcoder-based approaches that represent alternative paradigms, and (e) certified/robust circuit methods that address the same robustness concern from a different angle. The related work that does exist (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC, CIRCUS) is described but not critically compared in terms of how they inform the present study's limitations.\n\n### Overall Recommendation: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** (I am confident in my assessment, having reviewed the related literature carefully, though I am uncertain about which experimental results are real vs. simulated.)\n\n**Rationale:** This is a well-motivated study addressing a real gap: no one has systematically measured cross-method agreement in circuit discovery. The negative finding is valuable. However, the paper does not clear the top-venue bar for three reasons: (1) the would-be central quantitative contribution (Jaccard predicts CSS utility) lacks statistical significance; (2) the novelty of \"consensus needs independence\" is limited given established ensemble theory; and (3) the related work is severely underdeveloped. The confound analysis (ACDC `isinf` + zero-ablation) is the strongest contribution and could form the core of a focused short paper or Findings-track submission. To reach Main track, the paper needs either more data points to establish the Jaccard-utility relationship at significance, or a reframe that centers the confound analysis and diagnostic guidelines as the primary contribution rather than CSS itself."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7c0d32945c92e2a5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7c0d32945c92e2a5' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85189\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 129099</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36c8d43b-79cf-4a56-8499-4a4c6523d7de","uuid":"e2e156cb-1b11-4d14-980f-b2f6d95c5a99","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:37:02.461Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper\n\"When Does Cross-Method Consensus Fail in Circuit Discovery?\" — an empirical study of binary Consensus Support Scoring (CSS) across circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC, IFR) on 4 tasks × 4 transformer models. Central finding: CSS fails in 5/6 task–model configs because methods are not independent. Two confounds identified: ACDC isinf binarization + zero-ablation bias.\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages + tables)\n- §1 Intro: CSS formalized, central negative finding stated upfront\n- §2 Background: 4 methods described (EAP-IG gradient-based, EP gradient-based pruning, ACDC activation patching, IFR feature removal). CSS = fraction of K methods including edge. Jaccard for independence.\n- §3 Setup: 4 tasks (IOI, SVA, Arithmetic, DocQA), 4 models (GPT-2 117M → Llama-3.1 8B), top-k=100, 3 ablation regimes\n- §4 Results: \n  - GPT-2: CSS fails 3/4 tasks, only IOI positive\n  - Scaling: IFR replaces ACDC on larger models, still correlated (Jaccard 0.40-0.52), CSS advantage vanishes\n  - Confound analysis: ACDC binarization (Table 7) + zero-ablation bias (Table 8)\n  - Binarization sensitivity: CSS stable across k\n  - CIRCUS negative control: always fails\n  - Jaccard–utility: ρ=-0.54, p=0.27, n=6 — not significant\n- §5 Discussion: why methods correlate (shared gradients, privileged basis), IOI exception, fixed-k doesn't scale, 4 practical recommendations\n- §6 Related Work: ACDC [?], EAP-IG [?], EP [?], cross-method comparison [?], CIRCUS [?], ensemble circuit discovery [?] — ALL citations are [?] placeholders\n- §7 Conclusion\n\n## Key Claims\n1. CSS fails when methods are correlated (5/6 configs)\n2. Method diversity (Jaccard) directionally predicts CSS utility but statistically non-significant\n3. Two specific confounds identified: ACDC isinf + zero-ablation bias\n4. Practical guidelines: check independence first, use mean/resample ablation, normalize ACDC, report K_eff\n\n## Your Review Criteria (6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; top venue ~25% accept rate)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the negative finding (consensus fails when methods correlate) surprising or already known/expected? Is the confound analysis novel?\n2. **Contribution significance**: For a top venue, is \"CSS doesn't work well\" enough? What's the lasting contribution?\n3. **Related work completeness**: The paper cites methods but what about: (a) prior work on method agreement/disagreement in interpretability, (b) ensemble methods in other ML subfields, (c) theoretical work on when consensus helps, (d) concurrent work on circuit discovery benchmarks?\n4. **Framing**: Is \"analysis paper\" the right framing? Should it be positioned differently?\n5. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Are the negative results presented constructively?\n6. **Practical impact**: Will the 4 recommendations actually change how people do circuit discovery?\n\nProduce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions to authors\n- Novelty score (1-6) with justification\n- Related work score (1-6) with justification\n- Overall recommendation and confidence\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Negative-result / analysis papers have value — judge whether this one clears the bar for lasting contribution to the community.","agentId":"a7c0d32945c92e2a5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information to write a thorough review. Let me now compose it based on the paper description provided in the prompt and all the project materials I've reviewed.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether binary Consensus Support Scoring (CSS) across multiple circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC, IFR) can identify more faithful circuits than any single method alone. The central finding is negative in the majority of configurations: CSS fails in 5 out of 6 task-model combinations because the underlying methods are not independent, as measured by pairwise Jaccard indices. Two specific confounds are identified: ACDC's `isinf` binarization producing degenerate edge sets, and zero-ablation bias inflating or deflating CPR measurements. A Jaccard-based meta-diagnostic is proposed but the supporting correlation (rho=-0.54, p=0.27, n=6) is not statistically significant.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Timely and important question.** The field has produced many circuit discovery methods but virtually no systematic study of when and why they agree or disagree. This paper fills a genuine gap.\n\n- **Honest negative result.** The paper does not oversell: CSS fails in 5/6 configurations, and this is stated upfront. Negative-result papers in interpretability are undervalued, and the community benefits from knowing that naive consensus does not work.\n\n- **Confound analysis is concrete and actionable.** The identification of ACDC `isinf` binarization as a degeneration mechanism (Table 7) is a genuinely useful methodological contribution. This will save other researchers from making the same mistake. The zero-ablation bias finding (Table 8) corroborates Miller et al. (COLM 2024) with new evidence.\n\n- **Binarization sensitivity analysis** (CSS stable across k) is well-executed and addresses an obvious concern.\n\n- **CIRCUS negative control** adds value by showing that within-method threshold diversity also fails, reinforcing that the problem is deeper than aggregation strategy.\n\n- **Clear experimental setup.** Four tasks spanning the localized-to-distributed spectrum, four models from 117M to 8B, three ablation regimes. The experimental matrix is well-designed for the question being asked.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **[MAJOR] The claimed Jaccard-utility relationship is not statistically significant.** The paper reports rho=-0.54, p=0.27 on n=6 data points. This is the would-be \"crown jewel\" of the paper (Claim 2), but it does not clear even a lenient significance threshold. Without this, Claim 2 reduces to a \"directional observation\" that cannot support the proposed Jaccard threshold of ~0.53 or the two-step practitioner workflow. The paper needs either (a) many more data points to establish statistical power, or (b) to honestly downgrade this to a hypothesis rather than a finding.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Novelty of the central negative finding is limited.** The claim \"consensus fails when methods are correlated\" is, at the level of principle, unsurprising and arguably tautological. Any ensemble/voting system degrades when voters are correlated -- this is well-established in statistics (Condorcet's jury theorem requires independence), machine learning (ensemble diversity theory: Krogh & Vedelsby 1994, Brown et al. 2005), and has been noted in the interpretability context by Meloux et al. (2025), who showed single-method Jaccard of only 0.56 under hyperparameter variation. The novelty must rest on the *specific empirical quantification* rather than the principle, but the quantification is hampered by the non-significant correlation (see above).\n\n- **[MAJOR] Related work section is severely incomplete.** All citations in Section 6 are placeholder `[?]` markers. Beyond this formatting issue, the substantive coverage is thin:\n  - **No discussion of ensemble diversity theory** from the broader ML literature. The Jaccard-as-independence-predictor idea maps directly to the diversity-accuracy decomposition in ensemble methods (Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003, \"Measures of Diversity in Classifier Ensembles and Their Relationship with the Ensemble Accuracy\"). This connection is absent.\n  - **Missing concurrent/recent circuit discovery benchmarks:** InterpBench (NeurIPS 2024 D&B), MIB (ICML 2025), Formal MI (Hadad et al., ICLR 2026) all provide relevant context on method evaluation and ground-truth circuits. InterpBench specifically found ACDC and EAP-IG are \"statistically indistinguishable\" on semi-synthetic models -- directly relevant to the method correlation finding.\n  - **Certified/robust circuit discovery** is unmentioned: Anani et al. (2026, \"Certified Circuits\") and Nikankin et al. (BlackboxNLP 2025) on bootstrap EAP-IG stability both address circuit robustness through different lenses.\n  - **Circuit non-uniqueness** (Huang et al., ICLR 2025, \"Everything Everywhere All at Once\"): if circuits are inherently non-unique, the theoretical basis for expecting cross-method consensus requires explicit discussion.\n  - **SAE-based and transcoder-based circuit discovery** (Marks et al., ICLR 2025 Oral; Dunefsky et al., NeurIPS 2024; Ameisen et al. 2025) represent an entirely different paradigm for circuit discovery at the feature level rather than edge level. These are not discussed, yet they change the landscape of what \"cross-method consensus\" could mean.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Framing tension: the paper describes itself as a paper proposing CSS, but the actual finding is that CSS mostly does not work.** The title references CSS as an analysis paper's finding, but the introduction still reads as a method contribution (contributions list CSS as the first item). This framing mismatch will confuse reviewers. If the paper is an analysis/negative-result paper, it should be framed as: \"We systematically study whether cross-method consensus helps in circuit discovery and find that it mostly does not, due to method correlation. We identify two confounds and propose diagnostic guidelines.\" If it is a method paper proposing CSS, it needs CSS to work more often than it fails.\n\n- **[MINOR] The paper's 4 practical recommendations need validation.** \"Check independence first\" and \"use mean/resample ablation\" and \"normalize ACDC\" and \"report K_eff\" are sensible suggestions, but they are presented as guidelines without demonstrating that following them actually improves downstream circuit analysis outcomes. At minimum, showing that the one successful case (IOI on GPT-2) would have been predicted by the guidelines, and the five failures would have been avoided, would strengthen this claim.\n\n- **[MINOR] IOI as the sole positive case is concerning.** IOI is the most studied circuit in the interpretability literature (Wang et al. 2023), and methods have been iteratively tuned on it. The one case where CSS works (IOI) may reflect this privileged status rather than a general property of \"localized circuits.\" SVA is mentioned in the paper structure but the statistical evidence is based on n=6 with IOI driving most of the positive signal.\n\n- **[MINOR] IFR's role is not well-justified.** IFR is introduced as a fourth algorithmic family to increase diversity, but its theoretical properties (scoring edges by cosine similarity changes upon feature removal) are not clearly in the same \"causal circuit\" framework as the other three methods. The paper should discuss whether adding a non-causal method to a causal consensus framework is principled or introduces apples-to-oranges comparisons.\n\n### Questions to Authors\n\n1. The reported correlation is rho=-0.54, p=0.27 on n=6. In the project's \"best-tier\" imagined experiments, the correlation is rho=0.94, p=0.005 on n=6. Which numbers reflect the actual experimental results? If the former, how do you justify Claim 2? If the latter, please clarify which experiments were actually run vs. simulated.\n\n2. Have you considered the connection to ensemble diversity theory (e.g., Kuncheva & Whitaker's diversity measures)? Your Jaccard-based meta-diagnostic is essentially a diversity measure for circuit discovery ensembles. Framing it in those terms could strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. The ACDC `isinf` binarization confound effectively means ACDC is using a fundamentally different selection criterion than the other methods. After normalizing to top-k, what is the \"true\" disagreement pattern? Does ACDC still contribute independent information, or does it become redundant with one of the gradient methods?\n\n4. Why not include CD-T (Hsu et al., ICLR 2025), which represents a decomposition-based family distinct from all four methods used? This could genuinely increase K_eff.\n\n5. For the CIRCUS negative control: you ran CIRCUS on EAP-IG only. CIRCUS was designed for cross-layer transcoder attribution graphs (Ameisen et al.), not for EAP-IG. Is the negative result a fair comparison, or does it reflect applying CIRCUS outside its intended domain?\n\n### Novelty Score: 3/6\n\n**Justification:** The core negative finding (consensus fails under method correlation) is important to document empirically but is not conceptually surprising. The confound analysis (ACDC `isinf`, zero-ablation bias) is the most novel component but could be a short paper. The Jaccard-utility relationship, which would be the main novelty differentiator, is not statistically significant with the current data. Mondorf et al. (2025) already explored cross-method aggregation (union/intersection/mean) on gradient-family methods. CIRCUS (Parekh 2026) already explored consensus within single methods. This paper fills a gap between them but the gap is narrower than presented.\n\n### Related Work Score: 2/6\n\n**Justification:** All citations in Section 6 are literal `[?]` placeholders, which is unacceptable even for a draft. Beyond formatting, the paper omits (a) the ensemble diversity literature that directly motivates the Jaccard diagnostic, (b) concurrent circuit discovery benchmarks (InterpBench, MIB) that contextualize method disagreement, (c) the circuit non-uniqueness literature that challenges the theoretical basis of consensus, (d) SAE/transcoder-based approaches that represent alternative paradigms, and (e) certified/robust circuit methods that address the same robustness concern from a different angle. The related work that does exist (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC, CIRCUS) is described but not critically compared in terms of how they inform the present study's limitations.\n\n### Overall Recommendation: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** (I am confident in my assessment, having reviewed the related literature carefully, though I am uncertain about which experimental results are real vs. simulated.)\n\n**Rationale:** This is a well-motivated study addressing a real gap: no one has systematically measured cross-method agreement in circuit discovery. The negative finding is valuable. However, the paper does not clear the top-venue bar for three reasons: (1) the would-be central quantitative contribution (Jaccard predicts CSS utility) lacks statistical significance; (2) the novelty of \"consensus needs independence\" is limited given established ensemble theory; and (3) the related work is severely underdeveloped. The confound analysis (ACDC `isinf` + zero-ablation) is the strongest contribution and could form the core of a focused short paper or Findings-track submission. To reach Main track, the paper needs either more data points to establish the Jaccard-utility relationship at significance, or a reframe that centers the confound analysis and diagnostic guidelines as the primary contribution rather than CSS itself."}],"totalDurationMs":129099,"totalTokens":85189,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1209,"cache_read_input_tokens":81190,"output_tokens":2789,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1209},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2789,"cache_read_input_tokens":81190,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1209,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1209,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
